          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Protection Layers and Functional Safety",
          "content": "# Applied Project 05 \u2014 Protection Layers and Functional Safety\n\n## Scenario and objective\n\n**Scenario:** A high-level trip is credited alongside an operator alarm on a tank with a common level transmitter.\n\n**Target:** Separate prevention, control and mitigation; test independence assumptions in illustrative barrier and safety-instrumented-function calculations.\n\nThe task is a supervised conceptual study. It is not permission to change operating procedures, introduce hazardous chemicals, bypass protection or size relief devices. Assume that a multidisciplinary panel must decide whether to commission additional analysis, not whether this student workbook certifies a plant. Your deliverable must identify the claim being made, what evidence supports it, and what uncertainty remains. The board values clear red flags over cosmetic completion percentages.\n\n## Work package A: describe the situation\n\nDraw a block-flow boundary showing process inventory, incoming feeds or energy, manipulated variables, utilities, protective controls and relevant maintenance interfaces. Record the operating mode, unit conventions, material identity gaps and any location- or jurisdiction-dependent regulatory questions. Write one plausible hazardous event without guessing its annual frequency. For each asserted safeguard, identify what action it takes, when it acts relative to the top event, and which elements it shares with other protective controls.\n\nThe case provides a starting point only: **A high-level trip is credited alongside an operator alarm on a tank with a common level transmitter.**. Ask how equipment failure and incorrect operating information could combine. Check a routine operation, startup, shutdown and an interrupted utility condition. Treat any missing process safety information as a question with an owner; do not silently assume that a procedure exists or a transmitter is independent. Make a compact register of at least eight assumptions grouped into physical properties, instrumentation, human response and external conditions.\n\n## Work package B: model and verify\n\n**Lab 13 \u2014 [Illustrative barrier-chain success](../labs/lab-13.html).** Compute the baseline from `P(success of \u22651) = 1\u2212p_fail,1 p_fail,2` and export a one-parameter sweep. How would shared instrument power alter independence? The condition **Does not model common-cause, bypass, diagnostics or quality of barrier claims.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n**Lab 14 \u2014 [Conditional LOPA arithmetic](../labs/lab-14.html).** Compute the baseline from `f_residual = f_i PFD\u2081 PFD\u2082` and export a one-parameter sweep. Identify a shared-cause case where this figure grossly understates risk. The condition **Teaching illustration only. Never use for site LOPA, SIL assignment or risk acceptance.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n**Lab 15 \u2014 [Low-demand proof-test approximation](../labs/lab-15.html).** Compute the baseline from `PFD_avg \u2248 \u03bb_DU T / 2` and export a one-parameter sweep. Halve interval; explain why actual PFD may not halve when common-cause errors dominate. The condition **Small \u03bbT approximation; omits coverage, repairs, architecture and systemic failures; not IEC 61511 verification.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n## Work package C: argue the controls\n\nPrepare a bow-tie-style diagram with three credible threats and two possible consequence pathways. Describe at least one prevention design measure, one operating or inspection control, one possible protective-system layer and one mitigation measure. Do not treat all of these as independent or calculate a cumulative protection factor without justified evidence. Identify a shared-cause vulnerability (for example, common power supply, blocked sensing line or maintenance isolation) and show how your recommended verification would detect it.\n\nCreate a control register with columns **control purpose**, **covered initiating mechanism**, **physical element**, **mode of demand**, **availability evidence**, **testing method**, **owner**, and **unresolved dependency**. A valid control should have a documented performance claim that can be tested in the field. If field testing would introduce danger, propose a qualified nonintrusive verification method or documentation review instead of suggesting an uncontrolled trial.\n\n## Work package D: decision, uncertainty and follow-up\n\nDeliver a 600\u2013900-word decision memo with a recommendation of **further analysis**, **redesign for inherent safety**, or **insufficient evidence to proceed**. The answer must not state that a student's simulation proves safety or regulatory compliance. Begin with the most serious potentially unmitigated scenario. Include a one-page assumptions register and a prioritized list of the five most valuable additional data items. Differentiate uncertainty in inputs from uncertainty in the basic model structure. Show how one new piece of evidence could reverse your recommendation.\n\nThe expected project artifact is: **Never credit a layer twice, and never infer a real SIL target, safety integrity verification or tolerability decision from a slider.** Describe at least three acceptance tests, responsible roles and the evidence each would produce. Finish with a handover list for a qualified engineer or hazard-analysis team. A statement like \u201cwe will improve training\u201d is insufficient without specifying which error pathway it is intended to reduce and how the effect would be checked.\n\n## Assessment criteria\n\n- Process and time boundaries are explicit and aligned with the chosen hazardous event.\n- Equations reproduce the baseline, limits are visible, and CSV data agree with an independent computation.\n- Claimed safeguards have separate mechanisms and credible evidence; common-cause weaknesses are challenged.\n- Unknown chemical properties, actual frequencies and code requirements are not invented.\n- The conclusion is proportional to evidence and contains accountable next actions rather than a blanket approval."
