          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Mechanical Integrity, Human Factors and Change",
          "content": "# Applied Project 07 \u2014 Mechanical Integrity, Human Factors and Change\n\n## Scenario and objective\n\n**Scenario:** A replacement pump with nominally equivalent capacity changes its seal arrangement, spare parts and alarm configuration.\n\n**Target:** Analyze inspection intervals, deterioration, maintenance backlog, contractor interfaces, operational competence and management of change as a joined system.\n\nThe task is a supervised conceptual study. It is not permission to change operating procedures, introduce hazardous chemicals, bypass protection or size relief devices. Assume that a multidisciplinary panel must decide whether to commission additional analysis, not whether this student workbook certifies a plant. Your deliverable must identify the claim being made, what evidence supports it, and what uncertainty remains. The board values clear red flags over cosmetic completion percentages.\n\n## Work package A: describe the situation\n\nDraw a block-flow boundary showing process inventory, incoming feeds or energy, manipulated variables, utilities, protective controls and relevant maintenance interfaces. Record the operating mode, unit conventions, material identity gaps and any location- or jurisdiction-dependent regulatory questions. Write one plausible hazardous event without guessing its annual frequency. For each asserted safeguard, identify what action it takes, when it acts relative to the top event, and which elements it shares with other protective controls.\n\nThe case provides a starting point only: **A replacement pump with nominally equivalent capacity changes its seal arrangement, spare parts and alarm configuration.**. Ask how equipment failure and incorrect operating information could combine. Check a routine operation, startup, shutdown and an interrupted utility condition. Treat any missing process safety information as a question with an owner; do not silently assume that a procedure exists or a transmitter is independent. Make a compact register of at least eight assumptions grouped into physical properties, instrumentation, human response and external conditions.\n\n## Work package B: model and verify\n\n**Lab 19 \u2014 [Exponential component survival](../labs/lab-19.html).** Compute the baseline from `R = exp(\u2212\u03bbt)` and export a one-parameter sweep. Calculate R at two mission times; why could wear-out make the estimate optimistic? The condition **Failure rate constant; a hypothetical generic component is not a certified SIF.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n**Lab 20 \u2014 [Independent two-channel unavailability](../labs/lab-20.html).** Compute the baseline from `P(both unavailable) = p\u2081 p\u2082` and export a one-parameter sweep. Show effect of an unmodeled 2% common-cause path. The condition **No common cause, diagnostics, bypass or partial-stroke proof; not hardware architectural verification.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n**Lab 21 \u2014 [Proof-test coverage thought experiment](../labs/lab-21.html).** Compute the baseline from `Detected = coverage \u00d7 hidden defects` and export a one-parameter sweep. What does successful testing of 40 defects leave unknown? The condition **Illustration of coverage only, not a method to infer actual defect populations.** must appear as a red-line limitation in the report. Record at least one changed parameter set and reproduce one plotted point without JavaScript. Explain whether a favorable change in this model would genuinely improve the identified hazard or merely change one simplified quantity.\n\n## Work package C: argue the controls\n\nPrepare a bow-tie-style diagram with three credible threats and two possible consequence pathways. Describe at least one prevention design measure, one operating or inspection control, one possible protective-system layer and one mitigation measure. Do not treat all of these as independent or calculate a cumulative protection factor without justified evidence. Identify a shared-cause vulnerability (for example, common power supply, blocked sensing line or maintenance isolation) and show how your recommended verification would detect it.\n\nCreate a control register with columns **control purpose**, **covered initiating mechanism**, **physical element**, **mode of demand**, **availability evidence**, **testing method**, **owner**, and **unresolved dependency**. A valid control should have a documented performance claim that can be tested in the field. If field testing would introduce danger, propose a qualified nonintrusive verification method or documentation review instead of suggesting an uncontrolled trial.\n\n## Work package D: decision, uncertainty and follow-up\n\nDeliver a 600\u2013900-word decision memo with a recommendation of **further analysis**, **redesign for inherent safety**, or **insufficient evidence to proceed**. The answer must not state that a student's simulation proves safety or regulatory compliance. Begin with the most serious potentially unmitigated scenario. Include a one-page assumptions register and a prioritized list of the five most valuable additional data items. Differentiate uncertainty in inputs from uncertainty in the basic model structure. Show how one new piece of evidence could reverse your recommendation.\n\nThe expected project artifact is: **Change classification must consider chemical, technology, equipment, procedure, people and facility interactions, not just purchase price.** Describe at least three acceptance tests, responsible roles and the evidence each would produce. Finish with a handover list for a qualified engineer or hazard-analysis team. A statement like \u201cwe will improve training\u201d is insufficient without specifying which error pathway it is intended to reduce and how the effect would be checked.\n\n## Assessment criteria\n\n- Process and time boundaries are explicit and aligned with the chosen hazardous event.\n- Equations reproduce the baseline, limits are visible, and CSV data agree with an independent computation.\n- Claimed safeguards have separate mechanisms and credible evidence; common-cause weaknesses are challenged.\n- Unknown chemical properties, actual frequencies and code requirements are not invented.\n- The conclusion is proportional to evidence and contains accountable next actions rather than a blanket approval."
